{"results":[{"url":"https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/factcheck/2020/10/13/fact-check-harris-lesson-lincoln-supreme-court-nom-lacks-detail/5943183002/","final_url":"https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/factcheck/2020/10/13/fact-check-harris-lesson-lincoln-supreme-court-nom-lacks-detail/5943183002/","title":"Fact check: Harris' statement on Lincoln's Supreme Court nomination misses key details","description":"Harris skipped over the fact that Lincoln could not have nominated a replacement for a Supreme Court vacancy because Congress was not in session","language":"en","text":"# Fact check: Harris' statement on Lincoln's Supreme Court nomination misses key details\n\n## The claim: Harris' story about why Lincoln waited to fill a vacancy on the Supreme Court is wrong.\n\nThe question of when the late-Supreme Court Justice Ruth Bader Ginsburg's seat will be filled by a nominee of the president's choosing was up for debate immediately after her death last month. It was an unavoidable topic during the first vice presidential debate on Oct. 7.\n\nPresident Donald Trump has officially nominated 7th U.S. Circuit Court of Appeals Judge Amy Coney Barrett, a noted conservative, to the Supreme Court, USA TODAY reported. Barrett's confirmation would give the court a 6-3 conservative majority. Confirmation hearings began on Oct. 12, 22 days before the Nov. 3 presidential election.\n\nDuring the debate, Vice President Mike Pence asked whether Sen. Kamala Harris and Democratic presidential nominee Joe Biden would increase the number of justices, if they win the election.\n\nHarris responded with a story about President Abraham Lincoln's decision to delay a court nomination until after his 1864 campaign.\n\n\"Abraham Lincoln was up for reelection, and it was 27 days before the election, and a seat became open on the United States Supreme Court. Abraham Lincoln's party was in charge, not only in the White House, but the Senate,\" Harris said. \"But Honest Abe said, it's not the right thing to do. The American people deserve to make the decision about who will be the next president of the United States, and then that person can select who will serve for a lifetime on the highest court of our land.\"\n\nIt was a statement some disagreed with, including former House Speaker Newt Gingrich.\n\n\"Kamala Harris’ use of Lincoln to justify not nominating a Supreme Court Justice was totally false. The Senate was not in session and the minute it came into session Lincoln nominated a Justice. She is either really ignorant or just lied,\" Gingrich tweeted on Oct. 8.\n\nA screenshot of the tweet was posted to Facebook that evening. The user who posted the screenshot did not respond to a request comment from USA TODAY.\n\nOther social media posts similarly claimed Harris' story was wrong.\n\nIndeed, Harris' history lesson on Lincoln missed key details.\n\n## Did Lincoln delay filling a seat on the Supreme Court?\n\nPresidential historian Michael Beschloss tweeted on the night of the debate that Harris was right.\n\n\"Harris is correct that when Roger Taney died in October 1864, Abraham Lincoln deferred a Supreme Court appointment until after election so that next President, with a new mandate, could do it, Beschloss said.\n\nChief Justice Roger B. Taney, most famous for his majority opinion in the 1857 Dred Scott v. Sandford case, in which he wrote Black people were \"so far inferior, that they had no rights that the white man was bound to respect; and that the negro might justly and lawfully be reduced to slavery for his own benefit,\" died on Oct. 12, 1864, 27 days before the Nov. 8 election, as Harris cited.\n\nBut other evidence points to Lincoln being a savvy politician instead of a populist by deciding to delay a nomination for Taney's replacement before a major election.\n\nThe Lincoln administration considered options before the chronically ill chief justice's death, but the Civil War and a historic election factored into the timing of naming a successor, according to \"Abraham Lincoln’s Appointments to the Supreme Court: A Master Politician at his Craft\" by Michael A. Kahn. An excerpt is posted to the website for the President Lincoln's Cottage museum.\n\nLincoln was also willing to consider \"expressions of public opinion from the country” about the future nominee, according to historian Michael Burlingame, as reported in The Washington Post.\n\nBut of Harris' claim that Lincoln delayed the nomination to give Americans a say in the decision, Burlingame told PolitiFact, \"I've never seen anything like that quote in all my 36 years of Lincoln research.\"\n\nCandidates vying for Taney's position included ambitious climbers on both sides of the political aisle. Rather than alienate potential supporters, Lincoln used the Supreme Court vacancy as political leverage during his campaign, Kahn wrote. Influential politicians, such as former Treasury secretary, presidential candidate and rival Salmon P. Chase campaigned for Lincoln.\n\nCongress and the Supreme Court went into recess in July 1864 and was not scheduled to reconvene until Dec. 5, according to the U.S. Senate website.\n\nThough he won reelection, Lincoln did not put forth a nomination to replace Taney until lawmakers returned to Washington.\n\nGingrich correctly stated Lincoln submitted a Supreme Court nomination \"the minute\" it came into session. The president ultimately nominated Chase, his ardent supporter, on Dec. 6, 1864, apparently without telling any of his advisers.\n\n\"Lincoln could have announced his choice (after Taney's death),\" Kahn told USA TODAY. \"Whether he could have physically delivered it to the Senate is irrelevant.\"\n\nLincoln also wanted to avoid endangering his chances for reelection, according to Burlingame.\n\n\"Lincoln doubtless thought that it was inadvisable to summon a special session in the midst of a presidential campaign when his chances of reelection looked very good. Besides, members of Congress were busy stumping for their own candidacies,\" Burlingame told PolitiFact.\n\nUltimately, Kahn said, Lincoln and Trump were both faced with a choice of announcing their selection to the Supreme Court in an attempt to use it as a political advantage.\n\n\"Trump chose to announce a nominee to improve his political chances of being reelected. Lincoln had the same choice, but chose not to,\" Kahn stated.\n\n## Our rating: True\n\nWe rate this claim TRUE, based on our research. Lincoln delayed filling a vacancy on the Supreme Court that arose 27 days before the election of 1864. However, he did so not to defer to voters — as Harris stated — but because the Senate was not in session. He submitted a nomination a day after Congress reconvened.\n\n## Our fact-check sources:\n\n* USA TODAY, Oct. 8: \"Read the full transcript of vice presidential debate between Mike Pence and Kamala Harris\"\n* Journal of Supreme Court History, July 18, 2011: \"Abraham Lincoln's Appointments to the Supreme Court: A Master Politician at his Craft\"\n* President Lincoln's Cottage, Feb. 23, 2016: \"Lincoln's Election-Year Supreme Court Nominee\"\n* The Washington Post, Aug. 18, 2017: \"Removing a slavery defender’s statue: Roger B. Taney wrote one of Supreme Court’s worst rulings\"\n* The Washington Post, Oct. 8: \"Kamala Harris’s ‘little history lesson’ about Lincoln’s Supreme Court vacancy wasn’t exactly true\"\n* USA TODAY, Sept. 28: \"Amy Coney Barrett: Talented judge, popular professor brings solid conservative credentials\"\n* U.S Senate, accessed Oct. 10: \"Dates of Sessions of the Congress\"\n* USA TODAY, Oct. 4: \"Supreme Court hearings are moving forward, despite COVID-19 diagnoses for senators\"\n* Newt Gingrich Oct. 8 tweet\n* PolitiFact, Oct. 8: \"Kamala Harris jumped to conclusions about Abraham Lincoln’s delayed Supreme Court nomination\"\n* Michael Beschloss Oct. 7 tweet\n\nThank you for supporting our journalism. You can subscribe to our print edition, ad-free app or electronic newspaper replica here.\n\nOur fact check work is supported in part by a grant from Facebook.\n\nThis fact check is available at IFCN’s 2020 US Elections FactChat #Chatbot on WhatsApp. Click here, for more.","author":"Chelsey Cox","published_date":"2020-10-13T19:59:25Z","latency_ms":184.24027500441298,"format":"markdown"}],"errors":[]}